  "transcript": "Misrepresentation is giving a false or misleading account of a policy: its benefits, exclusions, premiums, dividends or the insurer’s financial condition. False advertising is the same deception in a brochure, website, seminar or ad. Telling Priya that a plan covers all prescriptions when it has a formulary, or calling a hospital indemnity policy health insurance, misrepresents. Omitting a material limitation can mislead as surely as a false statement. Maryland treats both as unfair trade practices under Section 27-202, with penalties, restitution and license discipline. The cure is the same standard from Lesson 10: say what the policy does, what it does not, and who stands behind it.\n\nCompetition is lawful; lying about competitors is not. Defamation under Section 27-204 is making or circulating a false or maliciously critical statement about an insurer’s financial condition that is calculated to injure its business. Theo may point out that a rival plan has a narrower network; he may not claim the rival is insolvent without basis. Section 27-205 prohibits knowingly false statements about an insurer’s financial condition and false or deceptive entries in insurer records, including altering records to mislead an examiner. Fabricating a client’s signature on an application or backdating a receipt falls here as well as under fraud.\n\nA rebate is anything of value given to induce a purchase that is not specified in the policy: returning part of the commission, paying the first premium, giving a gift card after the sale. Section 27-209 prohibits rebating and inducements. Maryland allows educational materials, promotional materials and merchandise costing no more than 50 dollars, but receipt may not be conditioned on buying insurance. A 20 dollar notebook handed to everyone at a seminar is lawful; the same notebook promised only to those who enroll is a rebate. Both parts of the exception, the dollar limit and the no-condition rule, must be met. Rebating is a violation for the producer and for a client who knowingly accepts it.\n\nTwisting is inducing a policyholder to lapse, surrender, replace, convert or retain a policy through misrepresentation or an incomplete comparison. Section 27-213 covers retention as well as replacement: telling Rosa her current insurer is failing to make her switch is twisting, and so is falsely telling her the new plan is worse to keep her from switching. An honest, complete comparison that leads to a replacement is lawful, as Lesson 14 explained. The difference lies in the accuracy and completeness of what the producer says. Medigap and long-term care replacement rules add their own notices on top of the general prohibition.\n\nPaying someone for referrals is lawful only within limits. A fee to a person who does not sell, solicit or negotiate may be paid only if it is not conditioned on a sale and does not operate as a rebate to the buyer; a payment that depends on the referred customer buying is commission sharing with an unlicensed person. Describing active sales work as a referral does not change its nature. Maryland also prohibits boycott, coercion and intimidation: agreeing with others to refuse to deal with an insurer, or pressuring a lender’s customers to buy insurance through a particular producer as a condition of credit, are unfair trade practices.\n\nWhen a question describes conduct, match it to the practice. A false statement about a policy to make a sale: misrepresentation. The same statement in a mailer: false advertising. A false claim that an insurer is broke: defamation. A forged signature or altered record: false entry. Cash or a gift tied to buying: rebating. A misleading comparison to move or keep a policyholder: twisting. A payment to an unlicensed friend that depends on the sale: unlawful commission sharing dressed as a referral. Each carries administrative penalties, restitution and possible license action, and several are also crimes."
